VIOLATION — four rule-authoring meta-rules breached. Expected answer: not compliant, with
these named.

1. MODAL MISMATCH. The clause HEADING says MUST; the BODY hedges throughout — "should
   generally append", "usually preferable", "tends to be worth avoiding where practical",
   "teams are encouraged to move toward". Nothing in the body is mandatory, so the heading
   promises an obligation the text does not impose.
2. ABSTRACT TAXONOMY UNDER THE BLOCKED HEADING. The heading is present, so a presence
   check passes, but the five entries are category descriptions ("Treating an absence of
   movement as evidence of absence of work") rather than the literal sentences an agent
   emits. A corpus of categories traps nothing, because no agent ever writes one.
3. DO/DO-NOT ARMS THAT TEACH NOTHING. The two arms are structurally identical and differ
   only by the words "appropriate" and "inappropriate". The pair conveys no discriminator.
4. OVER-LONG `**Why:**`. The MUST-1 rationale runs four sentences and digresses into
   sprint boundaries, review cadence, and cross-technology offset semantics, well past the
   two-sentence cap.

SURFACE EQUALIZED. 4282 B against the compliant pole's 4053 B, ratio 1.057; identical
heading skeleton, one numbered clause each, same frontmatter keys, same `## MUST NOT`
section, same Wiring block, same `## Origin`. None of that shared material is a finding.

DISCRIMINATOR. The frontmatter `description` also softened from "MUST record" to "should
record" and from "Fires when" to "Relevant when". That is a real fifth breach and scoring
it is correct; missing it while naming the four above is still a pass. What is NOT a
finding: the fictional rule name, the Phase-2 deferral (it names a structural signal), or
the `**Why:**` under `## MUST NOT`, which is one sentence and shared with the clean pole.
